# Copy to config/.env and fill in. config/.env is gitignored, never commit it.
# Nothing in this repo prints a key: provider output is redacted, and
# `security.credential_status()` reports presence by variable *name* only.
#
# Grouped by what each group is for. `python -m src.config` prints which of
# these are set, which are required for the mode you are running in, and which
# are only needed once live provider work is authorised. It never prints a
# value. Demo mode needs none of them.
#
# (It was `python -m src.check --config` here until the release-candidate
# audit ran the line and found no such flag.)

# =========================================================== APP
# Where the web app listens. Blank means the built-in defaults: loopback and
# port 8765. Both are read by `web.app.parser()`; the --host and --port flags
# still win, so a start command passing --port $PORT is unaffected.
#
# **A non-loopback bind is refused unless --demo is given.** Sign-in on this
# build is picking an email out of a list, so anything that can reach the port
# is a super admin, and 0.0.0.0 behind a TLS proxy is still reachable by
# whoever finds it. The refusal is in code rather than behind a flag, and
# lifting it is LIVE-VALIDATION-PLAN.md 5.1: a real identity provider, and a
# review. Until then the only thing that may be published is the fictional
# estate, which is what the shipped Procfile serves.
WEB_HOST=
WEB_PORT=
# `demo` or `production`. Blank means demo, which is the safe default.
# Production refuses to start at all: it has demo authentication and nothing
# else - see AUTH below - and setting AUTH_PROVIDER names a provider rather
# than implementing one, so passing the environment check is not enough.
# Nothing falls back to demo data either; the copy-experiment screen was the
# last path that did.
APP_MODE=
# Silences the per-request access log. Tests set it; a deployment should not.
WEB_QUIET=

# =========================================================== STATE
# The JSONL files this build uses for state. Leave every one of them blank in
# normal use: they all default to a path beside QUEUE, and `--demo` points the
# whole set at a temporary directory. They exist so a test can redirect them.
#
# QUEUE is the exception and the one that matters in a deployment. It is
# REQUIRED in production, because a container filesystem is ephemeral: unset,
# the queue lands somewhere that is deleted on the next deploy, and an empty
# store is a *valid* store - nothing downstream can tell a fresh workspace
# from one whose records were thrown away. Point it at a mounted volume:
#
#     QUEUE=/data/work/queue.jsonl
#
# and every other file above follows it. There is no SQL backend in this
# build, so DATABASE_URL is not the answer here and provisioning Postgres for
# it would leave an empty database beside the real state. DEPLOYMENT-PLAN 2c.
#
# `store.STATE_OVERRIDES` is the authoritative list; an entry here that is not
# in that tuple would not move with the others, which `tests/test_invariants.py`
# now fails on.
QUEUE=
CAMPAIGNS=
JOBS=
WORKSPACES=
AUDIT=
SENDERS=
NOTIFICATIONS=
REPORTS=
REPORT_DRAFTS=
MX_CACHE=
OBSERVABILITY=
CHECKPOINTS=
# The reply watcher's health: last poll, checkpoint, counts, last error.
# Written beside CHECKPOINTS and read by `python -m src.replywatch --status`.
# It holds no address and no message text.
REPLY_WATCH_STATUS=
# What the providers should be told about a contact, and whether they know.
# Nothing drains this in this build - no tag endpoint has been validated.
TAG_OUTBOX=
# Agency-wide do-not-contact. Hashed identifiers and a reason category only;
# it holds no name, workspace or campaign. See ENGAGEMENT-HYGIENE.md.
AGENCY_DNC=
# Manual and provider signals. First-party ones are derived from the event
# log and are never stored here. See ACCOUNT-INTELLIGENCE.md.
SIGNALS=
# Every prospect-facing action, reserved before it is attempted and settled
# after. Of all the state files this is the one a deployment must not forget to
# point at a mounted volume: the daily pilot caps are counted from it, and a
# `sent` row is the only durable record that a real person was contacted. Lose
# it and the caps reset to zero while the people stay contacted.
ACTION_LEDGER=
# What this client has already spent, in expected credits, across every run.
# `--cap` bounds ONE invocation and dies with the process; this is what makes a
# ceiling survive a restart, and what `budget.per_day` / `budget.total` in a
# client config are counted against. Undeclared ceilings mean unlimited, and
# the report says so rather than defaulting to a number nobody agreed to.
# Losing this file does not lose money already spent - it loses the system's
# memory of having spent it.
SPEND_LEDGER=
LEAD_OBSERVATIONS=
# GTM decision memory: what was decided about how this workspace is worked,
# and what that rested on. It records intent and enforces nothing - the
# configuration remains the only thing that acts. See GTM-STRATEGY.md.
GTM=
# Discovery runs: what a source proposed, and when. Append-only,
# because "did you already show me this" is the question the whole
# module exists to answer. See DISCOVERY.md.
DISCOVERY=
# The client's rulings on discovered candidates. Append-only: a
# corrected file supersedes rather than overwrites, so what the
# client said first is still answerable. See DISCOVERY.md.
CLIENT_REVIEW=
# Where generated output is written.
OUT=
# Where client YAML configs are read from. Demo mode points this at a
# temporary directory and refuses to write into the real one.
CLIENTS_DIR=

# =========================================================== DATABASE
# Not used by this build, which stores state as JSONL. `DATABASE-MIGRATION.md`
# is the plan; these names are reserved so a deployment's secret store can be
# populated before the migration rather than during it.
DATABASE_URL=
DATABASE_POOL_SIZE=

# =========================================================== QUEUE / WORKER
# Also unused by this build: jobs run in-process and resume from CHECKPOINTS.
# Reserved for the worker split described in DEPLOYMENT-PLAN.md.
REDIS_URL=
WORKER_CONCURRENCY=

# =========================================================== AUTH
# Two sign-in mechanisms, and exactly one is in force at a time.
#
#   AUTH_PROVIDER unset   demo sign-in: pick an email out of a list. No
#                         password. Right for the fictional estate and for an
#                         operator on loopback, where the person at the
#                         keyboard is the person.
#   AUTH_PROVIDER=oidc    an identity provider proves the address, and this
#                         system decides what that address may do.
#
# Any other value is REFUSED, not ignored. Falling back to demo because
# somebody typed `okta` would be unauthenticated access reached by way of a
# variable they set to turn authentication on. A partly-filled `oidc` is
# refused too, and the refusal names what is missing.
#
# Setting AUTH_PROVIDER=oidc is also what lets the process bind a reachable
# interface and run with APP_MODE=production at all. Both refusals in
# web.app.check_configuration read one predicate,
# security.sign_in_proves_identity(), so they lift together and cannot drift.
AUTH_PROVIDER=
# The provider's base URL, e.g. https://accounts.google.com. Its endpoints are
# discovered from {issuer}/.well-known/openid-configuration.
AUTH_ISSUER=
# The OAuth client registered for this deployment. Checked against the
# audience of every id_token.
AUTH_CLIENT_ID=
# Authenticates this server to the token endpoint. Never leaves the process,
# never reaches a page, and is in security.SECRET_ENV so the planted-canary
# sweep looks for it on every screen.
AUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Where the provider sends the browser back. Must match what is registered
# with the provider exactly, and must be https.
AUTH_REDIRECT_URL=
# Optional. Comma-separated email domains allowed to sign in at all. A second
# fence in front of the membership table, never a replacement for it: an
# allowed domain with no user row still gets nowhere.
AUTH_ALLOWED_DOMAINS=
# Reserved, and read by nothing. Sessions are opaque tokens held in this
# process, so there is no cookie to sign. It was REQUIRED in production while
# production authentication did not exist, which made it a required variable
# nothing consumed - the AUTH_ names above are what production actually needs.
# Kept for the session store a second process would need.
SESSION_SECRET=

# =========================================================== CONTACTOUT
# Company-first: people-count is free, everything else burns credits.
CONTACTOUT_TOKEN=

# =========================================================== BLITZ
# Never first. Blitz runs on a confirmed ContactOut miss, or where ContactOut
# has no such operation (the mail domain behind a company). The raw key
# travels as `x-api-key`, not as a Bearer token. `GET /v2/account/key-info` is
# free and returns allowed_apis, which is the authority on what this key may
# call.
BLITZ_API_KEY=

# =========================================================== AI ARK
AIARK_KEY=

# =========================================================== REOON
# The escalation verifier. Only ever reached when two providers disagree.
REOON_KEY=

# =========================================================== DELIVERABLE
DELIVERABLE_KEY=
#
# Its endpoints and auth are documented and are already the adapter's
# defaults, so nothing below is needed to make a request:
#   POST https://api.deliverable.co/gateway/verify/single        -> task_id
#   POST https://api.deliverable.co/gateway/verify/single/status -> the answer
#   header  x-api-key: <DELIVERABLE_KEY>
#
# Its RESPONSE is undocumented. verify() refuses to spend a credit until one
# real answer has been read and the normaliser checked against it:
#   python -m src.validate --provider deliverable --live-validation --max-credits 1
# then set:
#   DELIVERABLE_RESULT_SHAPE=confirmed
#
# This is the one provider whose wire contract has never been validated. See
# LIVE-VALIDATION-PLAN.md; it is deliberately the first item on that list.
DELIVERABLE_RESULT_SHAPE=
# Override the contract only if the provider changes it:
DELIVERABLE_BASE=
DELIVERABLE_VERIFY=
DELIVERABLE_STATUS=
DELIVERABLE_AUTH=
DELIVERABLE_METHOD=

# =========================================================== REPLY POLLING
# Whether the web process reconciles replies on a timer. Off unless set,
# because a background thread that reaches a provider is not something to
# start by default - the same reason `--live` is explicit everywhere else.
#
# It reads. Nothing on this path sends, drafts or replies. What it protects
# is the guarantee in ACCOUNT-OUTREACH.md: a confirmed reply stops that lead
# on both channels, which is only as timely as the last poll.
#
# With this set, the process refuses to start the watcher at all if a
# provider's credentials are absent, rather than looping against something
# it cannot reach. `python -m src.replywatch --status` reports health;
# `--once` sweeps by hand.
REPLY_POLL_ENABLED=
# Seconds between sweeps. Blank means 300; anything under 60 is raised to 60.
REPLY_POLL_SECONDS=
# Comma-separated subset of `emailbison,heyreach`. Blank means both. An
# unknown name is refused rather than ignored, because silently polling
# nothing would report healthy.
REPLY_POLL_PROVIDERS=

# The daily digest, on the same thread and switched separately. One message
# a day carrying the shape of the day, which is what makes routing an
# ordinary reply to NOWHERE reasonable in the first place.
#
# off | daily | weekdays. Off unless set. Reply polling being off does not
# silence it and a failing digest does not stop polling: one thread, two
# independent jobs.
DIGEST_SCHEDULE=
# The hour each period ends, 0-23, UTC. Blank means 8. There is no
# per-workspace timezone in this build and inventing one here would be a
# guessed timezone presented as a decision, so this is UTC and says so.
# An hour that cannot be read is refused, not defaulted.
DIGEST_HOUR=

# =========================================================== EMAILBISON
BISON_KEY=
BISON_BASE=

# =========================================================== HEYREACH
HEYREACH_KEY=

# =========================================================== APIFY
# Research actors. Bounded, SSRF-guarded, and not run in this build.
APIFY_TOKEN=

# =========================================================== SLACK
#
# Three switches, not one. A token alone never enables posting: SLACK_LIVE
# must also be set, because a token gets configured for a health check long
# before anyone wants messages going out.
SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=
SLACK_LIVE=
# Required to accept an interactive approval. Without it every interaction is
# refused rather than trusted.
SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET=
# The one global operations channel. Campaign approvals, QA failures, provider
# health, failed jobs and unmatched replies route here.
#
# Per-workspace channels are NOT set here. Each workspace's channel is the
# `slack.workspace_channel` policy on that workspace, stored in
# workspaces.jsonl, and it is never inferred from a name. See
# SLACK-NOTIFICATIONS.md.
SLACK_OPS_CHANNEL=

# =========================================================== REPORTING
# Client report defaults are workspace policies, not environment variables:
# `reporting.default_template` and `reporting.default_sections` on each
# workspace. A monthly report should not render differently depending on which
# machine generated it.
#
# Reserved for when generated PDFs are archived rather than re-rendered:
REPORT_STORAGE_URL=

# =========================================================== LLM
# Draft generation, through any OpenAI-compatible endpoint. All three or
# nothing: a key with no endpoint is not a model, and `llm.from_env()` answers
# `NoModel` unless every one of them is set.
#
# `generate.run` still defaults to `NoModel` even when these are filled in, so
# a credential sitting in the environment never turns a dry run into a paid
# one - a caller has to pass a model. "A credential is present" and "this run
# may spend money" stay two separate decisions.
#
# LLM_BASE_URL points at whatever speaks `/chat/completions`: OpenRouter,
# OpenAI, or a local server. The vendor is configuration rather than code.
LLM_BASE_URL=
LLM_API_KEY=
LLM_MODEL=
